---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths.js'

const packages = [
  {
    name: 'suitecut',
    kind: 'TypeScript · npm',
    text: 'Record, narrate, render and stream browser flows. Includes the suitecut command line and the suitecut-test runner.',
  },
  {
    name: '@suitecut/browser-control',
    kind: 'TypeScript · tarball',
    text: 'Drive the CEF engine, installed Chrome, Chromium, Edge or Brave over CDP, and Firefox over WebDriver BiDi, with a live viewer for human handover.',
  },
  {
    name: 'SuiteCut::Client',
    kind: 'C++20 · C ABI',
    text: 'A native SDK for the same browsers, with no Node.js, CEF headers or automation library.',
  },
  {
    name: 'suitecut-native-control',
    kind: 'Native executable',
    text: 'A command line with persistent sessions that also runs as an MCP server for agents.',
  },
  {
    name: 'SuiteCut CEF runtime',
    kind: 'C++ · CEF 156',
    text: 'The embedded engine itself, built from a pinned CEF and distributed separately from the SDKs.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Audio plugins',
    kind: 'TypeScript · branch',
    text: 'Optional local voices built on Sherpa: VITS and Piper, Kitten, Matcha, Pocket, Supertonic, ZipVoice and Kokoro.',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About · SuiteCut"
  description="What SuiteCut is, how its embedded Chrome works, and the packages it ships."
  current="about"
>
  <article class="page prose about">
    <h1>About SuiteCut</h1>
    <p class="about-lede">
      SuiteCut is an embedded Chrome built to be recorded, streamed and driven. It began as a way to
      turn scripted browser flows into narrated product videos, and grew into a browser layer that
      scripts, agents and people can share.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what">What we do</h2>
    <dl class="about-list">
      <div>
        <dt>Record</dt>
        <dd>
          Capture what the page really draws and plays, with a timeline of every narration,
          highlight and checkpoint beside it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Render</dt>
        <dd>Turn that timeline into a finished video with captions, zoom and mixed audio.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Stream</dt>
        <dd>Publish the same pixels and sound live to RTMP or RTMPS.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Drive</dt>
        <dd>
          Control the browser from TypeScript, C++, a command line or any MCP client, or point those
          same tools at the Chrome or Firefox you already have.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
    <p>
      SuiteCut ships its own Chromium Embedded Framework runtime, pinned to CEF 156. Each launch
      starts a sandboxed helper that hosts isolated contexts and pages. Video comes straight from
      the renderer's paint callback and audio from its audio stream, so there's no screenshot loop,
      browser extension or open debugging port in the capture path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Your code talks to the helper over a small binary protocol. Frames and sound flow back on one
      clock, where the recorder picks the newest real image for each output frame and never blends
      or invents motion. FFmpeg encodes the result; the renderer later composes it with narration
      into the final video, or the publisher streams it live.
    </p>
    <p>
      Browser control, the agent tools and the MCP server sit on the same native driver. That's why
      a script, a model and a person in the live viewer can all work in one session, with the same
      page, cookies and storage.
    </p>

    <h2 id="packages">Packages</h2>
    <div class="about-packages">
      {
        packages.map((item) => (
          <div>
            <p class="about-package-name">
              <strong>{item.name}</strong>
              <span>{item.kind}</span>
            </p>
            <p class="about-package-text">{item.text}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <h2 id="status">Where things stand</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Linux x64 and macOS ARM64 are qualified separately. Windows runtime packaging isn't
        implemented yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>suitecut</code> is published on npm. <code>@suitecut/browser-control</code> is built and
        packed from the repository and hasn't been published yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        Browser features are tracked family by family, with evidence and known gaps, in the
        <a href="https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main/docs/playwright-feature-parity.md"
          >feature checklist</a
        >.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every published <a href={sitePath('/benchmarks')}>benchmark</a> names its host, commit and the
        command that reproduces it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="open-source">Open source</h2>
    <p>
      SuiteCut is MIT licensed and developed in the open on
      <a href="https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut">GitHub</a>. Read the
      <a href="https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md">contributing guide</a> to
      get involved, and report security issues through the
      <a href="https://github.com/skyaara/suitecut/blob/main/SECURITY.md">security policy</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-lede {
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    font-size: 1.375rem;
    line-height: 2rem;
  }

  .about-list,
  .about-packages {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .about-list div,
  .about-packages > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 14rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .about-list dt {
    color: var(--ink-strong);
  }

  .about-list dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .about-packages p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .about-package-name {
    display: grid;
    align-content: start;
    gap: 0.35rem;
  }

  .about-package-name strong {
    color: var(--ink-strong);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .about-package-name span {
    color: var(--faint);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .about-package-text {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .about-list div,
    .about-packages > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }

    .about-package-name {
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
      align-items: center;
    }
  }
</style>
